 height from albedo or shape from shading for textures, monocular depth or surface normal estimation applied to textures).\n\nSECTION 2, example based texture synthesis and statistics that a procedural graph can match: Efros Leung, image quilting, Wang tiles, histogram preserving blending (Heitz and Neyret 2018), by example noise and Gabor noise fitting, spectral or power spectrum matching, Portilla Simoncelli texture statistics, Gram matrix or style loss, and how each could guide the choice of the NEXT layer.\n\nSECTION 3, comparison metrics for height maps: per pixel MAE and RMSE, SSIM and MS SSIM, LPIPS, gradient magnitude and orientation statistics (structure tensor, anisotropy), radially averaged power spectrum, histogram distances (Wasserstein 1D), black and white masks at absolute reference thresholds with IoU, connected component statistics (count, size distribution), profile correlation, and which ones are robust when the target contains random detail that a procedural noise cannot reproduce pixel for pixel. Give a recommended composite evaluation with numbers that separate deterministic structure (which can reach pixel level identity) from stochastic detail (which can only reach statistical identity).\n\nSECTION 4, official SideFX Copernicus resources for texture building (Houdini 20.5, 21, 22 release notes for COPs, new COP nodes, SideFX tutorials and HIVE talks on Copernicus texturing, SideFX forum and odforce threads about porting Substance Designer workflows, slope blur equivalents, tile patterns, flood fill, height maps), plus anything Adobe published about Houdini or Copernicus interoperability (Substance plugin for Houdini, sbsar in Houdini). The local SideFX page on Substance Designer equivalences is at /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/sidefx_substance_designer_equivalence.txt; the authoritative list of COP nodes of Houdini 22.0.368 is /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/cop_nodes_h22.txt. Read both.\n\nSECTION 5,