{"parentUuid":"bdcafdbb-b8ea-4bf1-aab8-ef89b1612675","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfvbVPnGg3tkwv2m27Acj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01GFd2LFcadKBvwkcmV3U3hZ","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cat /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/cop_nodes_h22.txt","description":"Read authoritative COP node list for Houdini 22"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":6482,"cache_read_input_tokens":212345,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":6482,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01GFd2LFcadKBvwkcmV3U3hZ":{"command":"cat /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/cop_nodes_h22.txt","description":"Read authoritative COP node list for Houdini 22"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"76a9f6b2-db4b-4a2c-b624-b461146d0a04","apiBlockIndex":2,"requestId":"req_011CfvbVPTBMYA9dyoLkLdbw","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"zenray-houdini","type":"assistant","uuid":"173e6f4f-28a6-4127-a6c3-dea5fb34007d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:19:15.305Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"bdcafdbb-b8ea-4bf1-aab8-ef89b1612675","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AeXzTu8viQUpYLUiic8vug","type":"tool_result","content":"= Copernicus for Substance 3D Designer(tm) users =\r\n\r\n\"\"\"Differences between using Substance 3D Designer and Copernicus.\"\"\"\r\n\r\n== Overview ==\r\n\r\nThis page is for users that are familiar with Substance 3D Designer(tm) and want to accomplish similar tasks in Houdini using Copernicus (COP). Though this page outlines differences between Substance 3D Designer and Copernicus, both have a dynamic, horizontal network that uses wires to connect nodes.\r\n\r\n:platform:Mac\r\n    Use ((Cmd)) instead of ((Ctrl)). See [/basics/macosx] for more macOS specifics.\r\n\r\n== Concepts ==\r\n\r\nIn Houdini, you add, connect, and organize Copernicus nodes in the Network editor. You can then configure a selected node's values in the Parameter editor. Use the [flags|Node:cop/index#flags] on COP nodes to control what's output in the Viewers. There are multiple ways to view your output in the Scene and Composite Viewers, so see [Viewing your output|/copernicus/working_with_cops#viewing-your-output] for more information.\r\n\r\nSubstance 3D Designer ||\r\n    Copernicus ||\r\n\r\nPackage |\r\n    A new Houdini instance loads as a .hip file. Instead of a package divided into elements, the [Network editor|/network/nodes] lets you set up multiple subnetworks. Houdini uses [recipes|/network/recipes] and [shelf tools|/shelf/index] instead of package templates.\r\n\r\nNodes |\r\n    Houdini networks are like a computer's file system, where networks are like folders and nodes are like files. Nodes can also be subnetworks that contain other nodes. You can also [reference nodes and their parameters|#parm_ref] in your networks.\r\n\r\nProject |\r\n    A [Houdini Digital Asset (HDA)|/assets/intro] is similar to a project made up of .SBS files in Substance 3D Designer.\r\n\r\nExplorer panel |\r\n    The network's hierarchy appears above the [Operation toolbar|/basics/ui#opbar] and [Network editor|/network/nodes]. You can also view this information in the [Tree View pane|/ref/panes/treeview].\r\n\r\nMain toolbar |\r\n    [Main menus|/ref/menus]\r\n\r\nProperties panel |\r\n    [Parameter editor|/network/parms]\r\n\r\n    Node-specific parameters appear in the Parameter editor of the node. Values considered __Attributes__ parameters in Substance 3D Designer appear in a node's [info window|/network/info]. Values considered __Base__ parameters in Substance 3D Designer appear in the Parameter editor for only COP Network nodes instead of for each node in the network.\r\n\r\nGraph viewport and toolbar |\r\n    [Network editor|/network/nodes] and toolbar\r\n\r\n2D and 3D viewports |\r\n    [Scene Viewer|/basics/view] outputs a volume and [Composite Viewer|/ref/views/imgview] outputs an image.\r\n\r\n    To display materials in your scene, see [Bringing COPs into LOPs for materials|/copernicus/working_with_cops#bringing-cops-into-lops-for-materials] or [Using COPs as materials|/copernicus/working_with_cops#copmat].\r\n\r\nLibrary panel |\r\n    Use the [Tab menu|/basics/tabmenu] to add [Node:cop/index].\r\n\r\nAssets library |\r\n    Install [Houdini Digital Assets|/assets/intro] that Houdini stores in a library file.\r\n\r\nFrame |\r\n    [Network box|/network/organize#netbox]\r\n\r\nSubstance function graphs |\r\n    [VOPs|Node:vop/index] and [/vex/index]\r\n\r\nGrayscale |\r\n    Mono\r\n\r\nLinks |\r\n    Wires\r\n\r\nBuilt-in variables |\r\n    [Variables and attributes|/network/expressions#variables-and-attributes]\r\n\r\n== Hotkeys and tips ==\r\n\r\nFunction ||\r\n    Copernicus ||\r\n\r\nConnect nodes |\r\n        * To connect a new node to the output of a selected node, press ((Shift + Enter)) after you find the node in the Tab menu.\r\n        * To connect a node between two nodes, drag the node onto the wire between the two nodes.\r\n\r\nCut wires |\r\n    Hold ((Y)) while you drag the mouse over the wire you want to cut.\r\n\r\n    You can also shake a node to disconnect all of its wires.\r\n\r\nSwap wires |\r\n    Select the node with the inputs you want to switch and press ((Shift + R)).\r\n\r\nReposition wires |\r\n    Drag a wire to a different input.\r\n\r\nChange wire style |\r\n    Press ((Shift + S)) in the network editor to change between straight and rounded wires.\r\n\r\nReset to default values |\r\n    ((RMB)) a parameter and choose __Revert to Defaults__.\r\n\r\nCreate anchors |\r\n    Hold ((Alt)) and click a wire.\r\n\r\nReframe nodes in the network |\r\n    Press ((F)) in the network editor. The network zooms to frame either the selected node or, if none are selected, all nodes.\r\n\r\nAlign nodes |\r\n    Press ((L)) in the network editor.\r\n\r\nTurn transparency on and off |\r\n    ((RMB)) in the Composite Viewer and turn __Display Options__ on. In the [Display Options toolbar|/ref/views/imgview#display-toolbar], click the __Toggle Transparency__ button on or off. \r\n        \r\n    You can also press ((Ctrl + Shift + T)) in the Composite Viewer.\r\n\r\nReferencing parameters |\r\n    You can copy parameters within the same node or across nodes.\r\n    # ((RMB)) the parameter you want to reference and choose __Copy Parameter__.\r\n    # ((RMB)) the parameter where you want to paste the reference and choose __Paste Relative References__.\r\n\r\n    See the following for more information about referencing parameters and nodes:\r\n    * [/network/copying]\r\n    * [Referencing COP networks|/copernicus/working_with_cops#referencing-cop-networks]\r\n\r\n    #id: parm_ref\r\n\r\nPromoting functions |\r\n    In the Parameter editor of the node you want to edit, click the [Icon:BUTTONS/gear] __Parameter Edit Menu__ button and choose [Edit Parameter Interface|/ref/windows/edit_parameter_interface].\r\n\r\n    The Edit Parameter Interface window is similar to the __Parameters tab__ in the Edit Operator Type Properties window, so see [Parameters tab|/ref/windows/optype#parms] for more information.\r\n\r\nInspect pixels |\r\n    ((RMB)) in the Composite Viewer and turn __Inpsect__ on. Hover over the image to view the pixel information.\r\n\r\n== Nodes ==\r\n\r\nThe following table outlines where to find certain Substance 3D Designer node operations in Copernicus. For more information, see [COP nodes|/nodes/cop/index].\r\n\r\nNOTE:\r\n    To search for nodes, press ((Tab)) in the Network editor or Viewer.\r\n\r\nSubstance 3D Designer ||\r\n    Copernicus ||\r\n\r\nAlpha Merge node |\r\n    [Node:cop/blend]\r\n    \r\n    Set the __Mode__ to __Add__ and wire an alpha into the `bg` or `fg` input.\r\n\r\nAmbient Occlusion (HBAO) node |\r\n    [Node:cop/heighttoambientocclusion]\r\n\r\nAuto Levels node |\r\n    [Node:cop/equalize]\r\n\r\nBase Material node |\r\n    [Node:cop/previewmaterial]\r\n\r\nBevel node |\r\n    [Node:cop/feather]\r\n\r\nDirectional Blur node |\r\n    [Node:cop/streakblur]\r\n\r\nDistance node |\r\n    [Node:cop/feather] and [Node:cop/extrapolateboundaries]\r\n\r\n    See [Distance node|#distance] for more information about how to configure these COPs so they act like a Distance node.\r\n\r\nDot (Portal) node |\r\n    [Node:cop/null] and [Node:cop/fetch]\r\n\r\nFlood Fill nodes |\r\n    [Node:cop/segmentbyconnectivity] and [Node:cop/uvmapbyid]\r\n\r\n    [Node:cop/randommono] and [Node:cop/randomrgb] nodes perform similar functions as Substance 3D Designer's Flood Fill to Random Grayscale and Flood Fill to Random Color nodes, respectively.\r\n\r\nGradient nodes |\r\n    Ramp nodes\r\n\r\nHistogram nodes |\r\n    Remap nodes\r\n\r\n    For example, set the __Operation__ to __Threshold__ to perform a similar function as Substance 3D Designer's Histogram Scan node.\r\n\r\nHSL node |\r\n    HSV nodes (where value replaces lightness)\r\n\r\nLevels node |\r\n    [Node:cop/remap]\r\n\r\nMake It Tile nodes |\r\n    [Node:cop/tilepattern] and [Node:cop/feather]\r\n\r\n    See [Distance node|#distance] for more information about how to configure these COPs to clean up the outlines of edges.\r\n\r\nMin Max node |\r\n    [Node:cop/statistics]\r\n\r\nOutput node |\r\n    [Node:cop/null] and [Node:cop/rop_image]\r\n\r\nPerlin Noise node |\r\n    [Node:cop/fractalnoise]\r\n\r\nRGBA Split node |\r\n    [Node:cop/channelsplit]\r\n\r\nShape node |\r\n    [Node:cop/sdfshape] wired into an [Node:cop/sdftomono]\r\n\r\nSlope Blur node |\r\n    [Distort by Slope|Node:cop/distort] (recipe)\r\n\r\nSplatter nodes |\r\n    [Node:cop/shapescatter] and [Node:cop/curvescatter]\r\n\r\nTile Generator node |\r\n    [Node:cop/tilepattern]\r\n\r\nTile Sampler node |\r\n    [Node:cop/shapescatter]\r\n\r\nUniform Color node |\r\n    [Node:cop/constant]\r\n\r\nWarp node |\r\n    [Node:cop/distort]\r\n\r\n== Use cases ==\r\n\r\nThe following are examples of how to set up certain COP nodes so that they work similarly to the relevant Substance 3D Designer nodes.\r\n\r\n=== Distance node === (distance)\r\n\r\nA common workflow in Substance 3D Designer is to make edges and then add those edges to a flood fill. This often results in ragged edges. As an alternative to their Distance node, you can use the Feather COP or Extrapolate Boundaries COP to clean up the edges' outline.\r\n\r\n==== Feather COP ====\r\n\r\nThe following is an example of how to clean up the outlines of edges with the Feather COP.\r\n\r\n# Create a [Tile Pattern COP|Node:/cop/tilepattern] in your Copernicus network.\r\n# Add a [Feather COP|Node:/cop/feather].\r\n# Wire the Tile Pattern COP's `tiles` output into the Feather COP's `source` input.\r\n# In the Feather COP, set __Feather Direction__ to `Circle`. This creates smoother results.\r\n# (Optional) Turn on the __Feather from High to Low__ parameter to expand the outer edges of the pattern shape into the background.\r\n# Adjust the __Distance for Unit Change__ parameter until you get the desired output.\r\n\r\nYou can also use the Feather COP to expand and shrink shapes.\r\n\r\n# Create a [Tile Pattern COP|Node:/cop/tilepattern] in your Copernicus network.\r\n# Add a [Feather COP|Node:/cop/feather].\r\n# Wire the Tile Pattern COP's `tiles` output into the Feather COP's `source` input.\r\n# In the Feather COP, adjust the __Distance for Unit Change__ parameter until you get the desired output.\r\n# Add a [Remap COP|Node:/cop/remap].\r\n# In the Remap COP, set __Operation__ to `Threshold` and __Threshold When__ to `Greater`.\r\n# Adjust the __Threshold__ parameter until you get the desired output.\r\n# Wire the Feather COP's `feather` output into the Remap COP's `source` input.\r\n# Turn on the Remap COP's display flag and select the Feather COP.\r\n# Turn the __Feather from High to Low__ parameter on to expand the outer edges of the pattern shape into the background or off to shrink the outer edges of the pattern shape inwards.\r\n\r\n==== Extrapolate Boundaries COP ====\r\n\r\nThe following is an example of how to clean up the outlines of edges with the Extrapolate Boundaries COP.\r\n\r\n# Create an [Extrapolate Boundaries COP|Node:/cop/extrapolateboundaries] in your Copernicus network.\r\n# Wire the design into the Extrapolate Boundaries COP's `source` input.\r\n# Wire the base shape into the Extrapolate Boundaries COP's `fillarea` input.\r\n    TIP:\r\n        To smooth small segments that can produce grainy artifacts, apply a [Blur COP|Node:/cop/blur] to the base shape before you wire it into the `fillarea`.\r\n# Wire the cracks into the Extrapolate Boundaries COP's `mask` input. This makes the blending stay within the cracks.\r\n# In the Extrapolate Boundaries COP, set __Exterior__ to `Unchanged`.\r\n# Adjust the __Threshold__ and __Edge Padding__ parameters until you get the desired output. See the following image for an example.\r\n\r\n    NOTE:\r\n        If your `fillarea` input is the inverse of your `source` input, you can adjust the Extrapolate Boundaries COP's __Edge Padding__ to make each dot's value expand outwards until it reaches the next expanding dot's edge. For example, you can do this to create cell patterns.\r\n\r\n    :fig: [Image:/images/copernicus/extrapolate_boundaries_distance.jpg]\r\n        #display: half\r\n\r\n== Next steps ==\r\n\r\n* [/basics/ui] for information about the UI\r\n* [/basics/handles] for information about how to navigate in the Scene Viewer\r\n* [/copernicus/intro] for high-level information about Copernicus\r\n* [/copernicus/glossary] for common Copernicus terminology\r\n* [Node:cop/index] for information about Copernicus nodes\r\n* [/copernicus/working_with_cops] for common Copernicus workflows","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"53135f6e-83c1-4256-82a4-a51d1bbf57e8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:19:15.750Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"76a9f6b2-db4b-4a2c-b624-b461146d0a04","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bdcafdbb-b8ea-4bf1-aab8-ef89b1612675","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
