{"parentUuid":"5c2b1be1-330f-4efe-8b6e-9298dcd937da","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VyTgZySPoNWUJZfW4Gqxyh","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MATch: Differentiable Material Graphs for Procedural Material Capture Shi 2020 arXiv\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Match: differentiable material graphs for procedural material capture\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/134067\"},{\"title\":\"Match: differentiable material graphs for procedural material capture\",\"url\":\"https://dspace.mit.edu/entities/publication/c1487622-b0ae-4002-9a59-4f26c9388494\"},{\"title\":\"cfg.mit.edu\",\"url\":\"https://cfg.mit.edu/publications/match\"},{\"title\":\"www.academia.edu\",\"url\":\"https://www.academia.edu/89089756/Match\"},{\"title\":\"cdfg.mit.edu\",\"url\":\"https://cdfg.mit.edu/assets/files/match.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"MATch: Differentiable Material Graphs for\",\"url\":\"https://match.csail.mit.edu/\"},{\"title\":\"cdfg.csail.mit.edu\",\"url\":\"https://cdfg.csail.mit.edu/publications/match\"},{\"title\":\"research.adobe.com\",\"url\":\"https://research.adobe.com/publication/match/\"},{\"title\":\"cdfg.csail.mit.edu\",\"url\":\"https://cdfg.csail.mit.edu/assets/files/match.pdf\"}]\n\nThe paper is **MATch: Differentiable Material Graphs for Procedural Material Capture** by Liang Shi, Beichen Li, Miloš Hašan, Kalyan Sunkavalli, Tamy Boubekeur, Radomír Měch, and Wojciech Matusik. It was published at SIGGRAPH Asia 2020 in ACM Transactions on Graphics. I didn't find an arXiv listing in these results, so the sources below are the publisher's DOI record, the authors' PDF, and project pages.\n\n**What it does:** The method takes photographs of material samples and turns them into procedural material models for production use. Its core is a library called DiffMat, which supplies differentiable building blocks for procedural materials and translates large procedural models into differentiable node graphs. A rendering layer then makes the whole pipeline end-to-end differentiable, so gradient-based optimization can adjust the parameters until the rendered result matches the target photo. The authors also propose a deep neural feature-based method for selecting graphs and initializing parameters, which lets the approach scale to many procedural graphs. The paper reports that it reconstructs more accurate and complex materials than prior state-of-the-art methods. It also produces arbitrary-resolution material maps and allows parametric editing of the appearance.\n\n**Sources:**\n- MIT DSpace record (includes the abstract and metadata): https://dspace.mit.edu/handle/1721.1/134067\n- Authors' PDF, ACM Trans. Graph. 39(6), Article 196, December 2020, DOI 10.1145/3414685.3417781: https://cdfg.mit.edu/assets/files/match.pdf\n- Project page: https://match.csail.mit.edu/\n- Adobe Research listing (publication date November 26, 2020): https://research.adobe.com/publication/match/\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"dd67f813-8743-483d-a4cf-9c459209dcca","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:20:25.501Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6c823960-c193-482f-8b33-d5d70ec14dc6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5c2b1be1-330f-4efe-8b6e-9298dcd937da","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"01dbb05e-b47c-4ef2-9a03-586ba0f0204e","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017ev4XCZhs5kqz4vSd9jp9H","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies\" Hu Guerrero Hasan SIGGRAPH 2022\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2207.07684\"},{\"title\":\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/node/233\"},{\"title\":\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/publications/node-graph-optimization-using-differentiable-proxies\"},{\"title\":\"node graph optimization using differentiable proxies\",\"url\":\"https://research.adobe.com/publication/node-graph-optimization-using-differentiable-proxies\"},{\"title\":\"node graph optimization using differentiable proxies\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/sites/default/files/node_graph_optimization_using_differentiable_proxies.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"skip to main content\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/biblio/10356278\"},{\"title\":\"par.nsf.gov\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/servlets/purl/10356278\"},{\"title\":\"adobe at siggraph 2022\",\"url\":\"https://research.adobe.com/news/adobe-at-siggraph-2022\"},{\"title\":\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies\",\"url\":\"https://cdnjs.deepai.org/publication/node-graph-optimization-using-differentiable-proxies\"}]\n\nThe paper is **\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies\"** by Yiwei Hu, Paul Guerrero, Miloš Hašan, Holly Rushmeier, and Valentin Deschaintre, published at SIGGRAPH 2022 in Vancouver, August 7–11, 2022. The author name is spelled \"Hašan,\" not \"Hasan,\" in the official listing. Adobe's research page lists the publication date as August 1, 2022.\n\n**Problem and approach:** Procedural material graphs are useful, but tuning them by hand is slow. The authors note that earlier graph-optimization methods were limited to optimizing differentiable functions in the graphs. Their framework instead targets graphs that contain non-differentiable nodes. It uses a differentiable approximator of a non-differentiable black-box function, called a Differentiable Proxy, so that end-to-end gradient based optimization of material graphs becomes possible. The method is applied to match structure and appearance of an output material to a target material, through a multi-stage differentiable optimization.\n\n**Where to find it:**\n- arXiv preprint: arxiv.org/abs/2207.07684 (PDF at arxiv.org/pdf/2207.07684)\n- ACM DOI: 10.1145/3528233.3530733\n- Yale graphics lab page and PDF, plus an NSF PAR record (the NSF record lists award 2007283)\n\nThe arXiv and Yale copies are the easiest to read in full. For the official ACM version, use the DOI above.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"2121fce6-db8b-410b-a4e7-aced16304204","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:20:28.708Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6c823960-c193-482f-8b33-d5d70ec14dc6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"01dbb05e-b47c-4ef2-9a03-586ba0f0204e","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f2b0d665-82b7-42ec-8a31-a0c297245c3c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YLrF5vNBi43rrUNMieURWe","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"\"A Novel Framework for Inverse Procedural Texture Modeling\" Hu Dorsey Rushmeier 2019\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"“A novel framework for inverse procedural texture modeling” by Hu, Dorsey and Rushmeier – ACM SIGGRAPH HISTORY ARCHIVES\",\"url\":\"https://history.siggraph.org/?p=213159\"},{\"title\":\"inverse procedural texture modeling high res 0\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/sites/default/files/inverse_procedural_texture_modeling_high-res_0.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"A Novel Framework For Inverse Procedural Texture Modeling\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/node/199\"},{\"title\":\"ipmm preprint\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/sites/default/files/ipmm_preprint.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Papers by HOLLY RUSHMEIER\",\"url\":\"https://independent.academia.edu/HOLLYRUSHMEIER\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2109.06395v2\"},{\"title\":\"an inverse procedural modeling pipeline for svbrdf maps\",\"url\":\"https://research.adobe.com/publication/an-inverse-procedural-modeling-pipeline-for-svbrdf-maps\"},{\"title\":\"yale graphics group siggraph asia 2019\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/news/yale-graphics-group-siggraph-asia-2019?page=1\"},{\"title\":\"skip to main content\",\"url\":\"https://par.nsf.gov/search/author:%22Rushmeier,%20Holly%22\"}]\n\nThe paper is by Yiwei Hu, Julie Dorsey, and Holly Rushmeier, all of Yale University. It was published in 2019 as \"A Novel Framework For Inverse Procedural Texture Modeling\" in *ACM Transactions on Graphics* as part of SIGGRAPH Asia 2019, vol. 38, no. 6 (A Novel Framework For Inverse Procedural Texture Modeling, ACM Transactions on Graphics (Proc. of SIGGRAPH AISA 2019), vol. 38, no. 6, 2019.). The Yale graphics group announced that Hu would present it at the Brisbane conference (Second year PhD student Yiwei Hu will be presenting his paper at SIGGRAPH Asia 2019 in Brisbane, Australia.).\n\n**Core idea:** The framework takes an exemplar texture image and works backward to find a procedural model and its parameters. Procedural textures offer user control, fast generation, low storage, and unlimited resolution, but building them by hand is slow (creating procedural models for complex textures requires a time-consuming process of selecting a combination of procedures and parameters). The authors address this by automating the selection and parameter estimation from examples.\n\n**Method, in brief:**\n- Textures are grouped into high-level classes, and each class gets its own inverse modeling system built from a collection of real-world textures and node-graph procedural models (For each high level class we build a data-driven inverse modeling system based on an extensive collection of real-world textures and procedural texture models in the form of node graphs.).\n- Unsupervised learning groups real-world images into sub-classes, and the outputs of each procedural model are assigned to those sub-classes (We use unsupervised learning on collected real-world images in a texture class to learn sub-classes. We then classify the output of each of the collected procedural models into these sub-classes.).\n- A convolutional neural network is trained for each procedural model to predict the parameters that reproduce a given output (For each of the collected models we train a convolutional neural network (CNN) to learn the parameters to produce a specific output texture.).\n- At use time, the system classifies the user's exemplar, picks the matching models, and runs their pre-trained CNNs to estimate parameters (The system first classifies the texture into a sub-class, and selects the procedural models that produce output in that sub-class. The pre-trained CNNs of the selected models are used to estimate the parameters of the texture example.).\n\n**Reported results:** The preprint says the generated textures can be expanded to arbitrary resolution such as 4K or 8K, with average run times of about 160 ms for 4K textures and 1.5 s for 8K textures (per the high-res PDF's figure caption on the Yale site).\n\n**Follow-up work:** Hu and colleagues later extended the idea to materials. Their 2021 pipeline for SVBRDF maps aims to create procedural models without relying on pre-existing artist-designed models, and the 2019 paper is cited as one of the first steps in this direction (Shi et al. 2020 took a first step by proposing different inverse modeling frameworks to select a procedural graph among existing models and optimize the nodes parameters to best match an input texture. is the related earlier method cited alongside Hu et al. 2019).\n\n**Where to read it:** The full text, a low-res version, and supplementary material are on the Yale Graphics group page (graphics.cs.yale.edu/node/199), and the ACM SIGGRAPH History Archives also has an entry for it.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"7d41ea65-1339-4faa-99e4-b0ba28b387d8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:20:29.382Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"6c823960-c193-482f-8b33-d5d70ec14dc6","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f2b0d665-82b7-42ec-8a31-a0c297245c3c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
