{"parentUuid":"0675e23e-0524-4b5e-b06a-2bbadf5966ab","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BVPVcTu59vGrsdvEbKM5hn","type":"tool_result","content":"**Abstract**\nMATch automatically converts photographs of material samples into procedural material models. It uses DiffMat, a library of differentiable building blocks, to translate large procedural models into differentiable node graphs. The page claims this yields more accurate, general, and complex materials than prior state-of-the-art methods, plus arbitrary-resolution material maps and parametric editing.\n\n**Method overview**\n- Procedural models with hundreds to thousands of node parameters are translated into differentiable node graphs with DiffMat.\n- These graphs are combined with a rendering layer, forming an end-to-end differentiable pipeline from graph parameters to rendered images.\n- Gradient-based optimization adjusts the parameters so the rendered material matches the target photograph.\n\n**Loss**\nThe optimization minimizes a \"style transfer loss\" that measures how closely the rendered appearance matches the target image.\n\n**Graph selection and initialization**\nThe page describes a deep neural feature-based method that selects a suitable graph and initializes its parameters. It is designed to scale to a large number of procedural graphs. The page gives no further detail on the features or the selection procedure.\n\n**Code (DiffMat)**\n- Actively maintained: https://github.com/mit-gfx/diffmat\n- Legacy: https://github.com/mit-gfx/diffmat-legacy\n\n**Limitations**\nThe page does not state any limitations, and it has no limitations section."}]},"uuid":"4bb6592c-5f52-47d7-b8ca-5c6ac8170c4d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:20:56.187Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"3923a1af-ee73-4866-adf5-2df3d4a0585a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","r