s an end-to-end capture framework that pairs gradient-based optimization with gradient-free parameter search. According to the abstract, the framework matches production-grade procedural materials to user-taken flash photos (combines gradient-based optimization and gradient-free parameter search to match existing production-grade procedural materials against user-taken flash photos).\n\nThe results include a related paper by the same group, \"Computational Discovery of Microstructured Composites,\" which has an arXiv entry (abs/2302.01078). That is a different work, so don't confuse the two.\n\nTo find an arXiv version, you could search arXiv directly for the title, or check the authors' publication page at the MIT CSAIL site, which hosts a PDF of the paper.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"baa8190b-50b0-4bf8-9e65-4c4e6b1b3d5a","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:21:40.020Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"73eb124c-0b55-4d5e-8e4d-488e6c04baa1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"bfc5ac75-22bf-4170-9849-6ae27a12f099","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"d75157d1-d2c2-4e7f-a3fc-3f72ed1a24a7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Dy68vaKBpYv5jwQ6bJ1ZPg","type":"tool_result","content":"**Abstract (paraphrased):** The paper introduces MATch, a method that turns photographs of material samples into production-grade procedural material models. It uses DiffMat, a new library of differentiable building blocks that converts large procedural models, with hundreds to thousands of parameters, into differentiable node graphs. Combined with a rendering layer, this creates an end-to-end pipeline that gradient-based optimization can fit to a target photo using a style transfer loss. The authors also use deep neural features to select graphs and initialize parameters efficiently. Tests on synthetic and real flash photographs show more accurate and complex results than prior methods, and the procedural output supports arbitrary-resolution material maps and parametric editing.\n\n**Direct PDF bitstream link:** `/bitstreams/34fc9770-5e54-4574-b6f0-207efb9c1384/download`\n\nThis is a relative path on the MIT Open Access DSpace site, so prepend the repository's domain to get the full URL. The file is \"3414685.3417781.pdf\" (Published version, 24.41 MB). The persistent item link is https://hdl.handle.net/1721.1/134067."}]},"uuid":"008b8ac9-a79e-4f0c-ac34-1f12dcf62532","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:21:41.078Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"73eb124c-0b55-4d5e-8e4d-488e6c04baa1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d75157d1-d2c2-4e7f-a3fc-3f72ed1a24a7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"cla