oint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"fb897dac-da79-4ff0-815f-f4236a7853ae","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VBSgo1sMxLgyPMnKZnzsjZ","type":"tool_result","content":"**1. Graph representation**\n- A material is a directed acyclic multigraph of nodes and edges. Each node has an operator type τ and parameters P.\n- Nodes are generated as a sequence of operator types, with start and stop tokens.\n- Parameters are a flattened sequence of scalar values per node, sorted alphabetically by name. Only non-default values are included, with a companion index sequence.\n- Continuous parameters are quantized to 32 levels.\n- Edges are a sequence of slot indices. Each consecutive pair forms one edge from an output slot to an input slot.\n\n**2. Model architecture (three stages, each a GPT-2-style transformer)**\n- **Node transformer:** generates operator types and node depth.\n- **Parameter transformer:** generates parameter sequences, conditioned on an encoded embedding of the node type.\n- **Edge generator:** a transformer with a pointer network. It selects slots by dot-product affinity with learned slot embeddings.\n- Each model uses 2 attention layers, 4 heads per layer, and 64-dimensional hidden features (as listed in Appendix D).\n\n**3. Training data**\n- A subset of the Substance Source dataset: about 4k graphs, filtered to about 2.8k.\n- Augmented by perturbing each parameter in [0.8v, 1.2v], producing about 280k graphs.\n- Validation set: 500 graphs from 5 original graphs.\n\n**4. Semantic validity enforcement**\n- At inference, probabilities of invalid choices are set to zero and the rest are renormalized.\n- Edges must go from output slots to input slots.\n- Edge endpoints cannot be ancestors of the start node, which prevents cycles.\n- Discrete parameter values stay in valid ranges, and parameter indices stay within the operator's parameter count.\n- No validity checks are applied in the node generation step.\n\n**5. Evaluation metrics**\n- Graph statistics (EMD over histograms): 0.046 for full MatFormer (lower is better).\n- FID on rendered materials: 48.6 (lower is better).\n- Normalized nearest-neighbor style distance: 1.01 (values near 1.0 are ideal).\n- Normalized nearest-neighbor graph edit distance: 1.34, for graphs with at least 50 nodes.\n- User study (two artists, 0–5 usability scale): 1.82 for MatFormer versus 0.35 (macro-nodes) and 0.48 (pairwise statistics).\n\n**6. Limitations**\n- The paper states: \"MatFormer is constrained by the diversity of the procedural graphs it is trained on.\"\n- It cannot yet condition generation on a target image.\n- Parameter and edge generation are independent of each other.\n- Training excluded graphs with complex parameterization, such as nodes using pixel shaders.\n- Outputs lack semantic control handles and hierarchical structure.\n- The user study involved only two artists."}]},"uuid":"a05f9613-0681-4243-a04d-388a6aa32d48","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:12.655Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1f9b3cdb-4294-4fc0-a51c-acf18c39b527","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"fb897dac-da79-4ff0-815f-f4236a7853ae","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"8d146b9c-a1a3-423e-864b-9fab9f240b78","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GCUZnQ53S3KHMCq2dc5gS7","type":"tool_result","content":"**(1) Graph representation and tokenization**\n- A material graph is a directed acyclic graph of nodes n_i = (τ_i, P_i), where τ_i is the operation type and P_i its parameters. Edges connect output slots to input slots.\n- The graph is linearized into three sequences: a node sequence S_n, an edge sequence S_e of slot pointers (Pointer Network head), and a single parameter sequence S_p covering all parameters.\n- Quantization: \"We use 128 quantization bins (compared to the 32 used by MatFormer).\"\n\n**(2) Conditioning**\n- A frozen CLIP ViT-L/14 encodes the input.\n- Text prompts: the CLIP text embedding goes through a prior (DALL·E 2-style, trained on 10M text/texture pairs) that maps it to a CLIP texture-image embedding.\n- A trainable MLP maps the embedding to the transformer hidden size. It is added to each transformer encoder layer's output after layer normalization.\n- An alternative encoder (Ours (VGG)) adds VGG feature statistics and a 16×16 thumbnail, but it cannot encode text.\n\n**(3) Training data**\n- Source: Substance Source. The raw graphs were inconsistent across system versions, so they were cleaned and reformatted.\n- Filtering removes graphs with more than 80 nodes, 200 edges, or 210 slots. The result is 4,667 graphs (72% of the unfiltered set; MatFormer had 2,820).\n- Augmentation: 100 parameter sets per graph, sampled from Gaussians around preset defaults, giving 466,700 graphs.\n- Train/validation split is done before augmentation, so the splits differ in topology.\n\n**(4) Generation pipeline**\n- Node transformer, then edge transformer (Pointer Network head), then parameter transformer. The parameter model is conditioned on S_n and S_e and uses a 6-layer GCN for edge context.\n- Hidden size / layers / heads: node 40/2/4, edge 48/2/4, parameter 96/4/4.\n- Sampling: nucleus filtering with top-p = 0.9, \"sample the top-5 candidates,\" no temperature adjustment, and semantic validity checks during decoding.\n- Inference takes about 1.32 s per graph.\n\n**(5) Post-generation optimization**\n- The paper does not mention DiffMat. It cites MATch (Shi et al. 2020) and Hu et al. 2022a for differentiable optimization.\n- The top-k = 5 graphs, ranked by CLIP cosine or sliced Wasserstein distance, are optimized. Only optimizable nodes are refined, so topology is fixed.\n- Table 1 describes the optimization as using \"style loss,\" defined as L1 on VGG Gram matrices plus 0.1 × L1 on 16×16 thumbnails. The paper does not state the optimizer loss explicitly.\n- Cost: about 2–3 minutes per graph on an RTX 3090.\n\n**(6) Evaluation metrics**\n- Style loss, reported as Best-of-5 and Average-of-5 over the top-5 samples, on 48 real photos.\n- Retrieval ranking uses CLIP cosine or sliced Wasserstein distance. LPIPS is not used.\n- Optimized Best-of-5: Ours 0.0218, Ours (VGG) 0.0199, Ours Uncond 0.0194, Dataset 0.0191. The paper describes these as \"similar or better\" performance.\n\n**(7) Stated limitations**\n- Limited data. Each base graph is handcrafted by an artist.\n- Only standard PBR maps are supported (Base Color, Normal, Roughness, Metalness).\n- Quantization errors remain, and details can mismatch the input (Fig. 9).\n- Text-conditioned graphs do not always look realistic or match the prompt (Fig. 11).\n- Optimization only adjusts parameters and cannot change topology."}]},"uuid":"201b0b99-b118-49f1-8b9a-4e2464105173","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:19.374Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"1f9b3cdb-4294-4fc0-a51c-acf18c39b527","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platf