38788520ec54e4b4-Paper-Conference.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Interpretable procedural material graph generation via diffusion models from reference images\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00371-025-04096-0\"},{\"title\":\"Generating Procedural Materials from Text or Image Prompts\",\"url\":\"https://www.researchgate.net/publication/372549047_Generating_Procedural_Materials_from_Text_or_Image_Prompts\"},{\"title\":\"Generating Procedural Materials from Text or Image Prompts\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/publications/generating-procedural-materials-text-or-image-prompts\"},{\"title\":\"Generating Procedural Materials from Text or Image Prompts\",\"url\":\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/fullHtml/10.1145/3588432.3591520\"},{\"title\":\"Procedural Material - Paper Copilot\",\"url\":\"https://papercopilot.com/cat/cs/cg/materials-and-textures/material-representation/procedural-material/\"},{\"title\":\"Node Graph Optimization Using Differentiable Proxies — Lacuna\",\"url\":\"https://lacuna.tiptreesystems.com/work/node-graph-optimization-using-differentiable-proxies/wrk_d20eb86db8f6cf7bc10d5a236a7a88fd\"},{\"title\":\"Generating Procedural Materials from Text or Image Prompts\",\"url\":\"https://graphics.cs.yale.edu/node/250\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22151v2\"}]\n\nI didn't find a paper that matches all of your criteria: an LLM agent, procedural material graph generation from an image, published at SIGGRAPH 2025. The closest matches are from other venues or years.\n\n**Closest 2025 work (not SIGGRAPH)**\n- **VLMaterial (ICLR 2025):** This paper fine-tunes a vision-language model to output procedural materials as Python programs, which can be converted from Blender node graphs. It prompts another pre-trained large language model as part of its pipeline, and it is the most direct match to \"LLM generates material graph from image.\" It also notes that previous works are developed for proprietary software, e.g., Adobe Substance 3D, which is why the authors built an open-source Blender dataset.\n- **Diffusion-based graph generation (The Visual Computer, July 2025):** Lv et al. predict procedural nodes with an adapter-based diffusion model, forming an auxiliary graph, which is then refined using a DiffMat-based node parameter optimization method. This is a diffusion model, not an LLM agent, and it is published in a journal.\n- **MultiMat (ICLR 2026):** This is a later paper on multimodal program synthesis for procedural materials using large multimodal models, with Adobe Research among the authors. It is listed as ICLR 2026, so it is outside the 2025 window.\n\n**Related SIGGRAPH work (earlier years)**\n- **Generating Procedural Materials from Text or Image Prompts (SIGGRAPH 2023):** This is the closest SIGGRAPH match for node-graph generation from images. It proposes a multi-modal node graph generation neural architecture for high-quality procedural material synthesis which can be conditioned on different inputs (text or image prompts), using a CLIP-based encoder.\n- **MatFormer (SIGGRAPH 2022):** This paper presents a generative model for procedural materials and is an earlier foundation for this line of work.\n\n**Assessment:** If you saw a SIGGRAPH 2025 paper that combines an LLM agent with Substance-style graphs, I could not confirm it from these results. Searching the SIGGRAPH 2025 technical papers program or the ACM Digital Library directly for \"procedural material\" would be the most reliable check. The results also suggest that the Substance-to-Python or Blender-to-Python approach is a common direction, so the paper you're thinking of may be described differently, for example as \"shader graph,\" \"node graph,\" or \"neural program synthesis.\"\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"4bdea65f-cd96-4450-a255-d0eb307360c8","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:32.266Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"