{"parentUuid":"be64b401-d6b7-4dd6-935a-424bb4784139","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JNjN3DMKKj7RMygBJBTZGv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"MultiMat multimodal program synthesis procedural materials ICLR 2026 arXiv\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"MultiMat: Multimodal Program Synthesis for Procedural Materials using Large Multimodal Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22151\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22151v2\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.22151v1\"},{\"title\":\"research.adobe.com\",\"url\":\"https://research.adobe.com/publication/multimat\"},{\"title\":\"MultiMat: Multimodal Program Synthesis for Procedural Materials using Large Multimodal Models\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2509.22151v1\"},{\"title\":\"iclr.cc\",\"url\":\"https://iclr.cc/media/iclr-2026/Slides/10009540.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.22151v1\"},{\"title\":\"MultiMat: Multimodal Program Synthesis for Procedural Materials Using Large Multimodal Models\",\"url\":\"https://mlanthology.org/iclr/2026/belouadi2026iclr-multimat/\"},{\"title\":\"MultiMat: Multimodal Program Synthesis for Procedural Materials using Large Multimodal Models\",\"url\":\"https://chatpaper.com/es/paper/192570\"}]\n\nThe paper is MultiMat, by Jonas Belouadi, Tamy Boubekeur, and Adrien Kaiser (University of Mannheim and Adobe Research). It was accepted to ICLR 2026 and is on arXiv as 2509.22151.\n\n**Core idea:** Procedural materials are built as node graphs, and writing them is hard. The authors argue that text-only models lack the visual feedback a human artist uses. They propose a multimodal program synthesis paradigm based on large multimodal models that incorporates visual feedback throughout the generation process. MultiMat processes both visual and textual graph representations to improve generation of procedural material graphs.\n\n**Method:** The models are trained on a new dataset of production-quality procedural materials and combined with a constrained tree search inference algorithm. The ICLR slides describe the model as iteratively generating nodes in topological order while updating its context with visual representations of intermediate states.\n\n**Results:** The authors report higher visual quality and fidelity than text-only baselines in both unconditional and conditional graph synthesis, which they describe as new state-of-the-art performance.\n\n**Publication status:** The arXiv abstract page notes the paper was submitted to ICLR 2026. The arXiv listing shows version 1 from 26 September 2025 and version 2 from 9 February 2026, and the v2 PDF is marked \"Published as a conference paper at ICLR 2026.\" The MLAnthology entry gives the citation as the International Conference on Learning Representations, 2026.\n\n**Minor discrepancy:** The v1 abstract describes the search as ensuring syntactic validity, while the MLAnthology version says static correctness. The Adobe research page lists a publication date of April 25, 2026.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"8e2bd735-c6cd-4dde-9bea-6bdc4fb0a171","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:43.977Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"294b8496-94c3-4b16-8dee-f04c1fae6064","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"be64b401-d6b7-4dd6-935a-424bb4784139","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90