{"parentUuid":"3917a182-2a1d-4b2f-a630-43f5e22b0c0c","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011snwooWuZY8GFUY33k3KVx","type":"tool_result","content":"**1. Program representation:** Blender node graphs are converted to Python. The paper says \"procedural material graphs in Blender ... can be transpiled into standard Python programs.\" Each program defines a `shader_material(material)` function that creates nodes, links, and parameters. Graphs are capped at 30 nodes, and programs at 2,048 LLaMA 3 tokens.\n\n**2. VLM and fine-tuning:** LLaVA-NeXT with a CLIP ViT-L/14 vision encoder, a LLaMA 3 8B decoder, and an MLP projector. Training uses LoRA (r=8, α=32, dropout 0.05), about 40M trainable parameters, and AdamW at 1e-4 with cosine annealing and 3% warmup. Training ran in BF16 on 8× H100 80GB GPUs with DeepSpeed ZeRO-3 for 5 epochs over three days.\n\n**3. Dataset and LLM augmentation:** The authors collected 3,663 free Blender materials, and about 1.6K remained after curation. GPT-4o-mini performed \"genetic crossovers\" on a 870-material pool, producing 50.4K structurally distinct programs. Parameter augmentation added roughly 10× more variations. The final dataset has over 550K materials.\n\n**4. Inference and post-optimization:** The model generates at most N=50 programs and stops after K=20 valid ones. The program with the lowest style loss against the input is selected. Post-optimization is a gradient-free MCMC local parameter search, not gradient-based. It runs 200 iterations with a 0.05 acceptance probability for worse samples. Table 1 reports results before post-optimization.\n\n**5. Evaluation metrics:** The paper uses style loss, SWD (sliced Wasserstein distance), and CLIP cosine similarity. It also reports program correctness (valid programs divided by attempts). DreamSim and LPIPS are not mentioned in the provided content.\n\n**6. Limitations:**\n- Intricate textures can exceed the expressiveness of Blender node types within the 30-node limit, so the model may fail to predict a good graph structure.\n- Program correctness declines for materials with more than 22 nodes or 2,000 tokens.\n- Simple materials with fewer than 4 nodes show worse style loss and correctness.\n- Under a tighter budget (N=20, K=4), real-image style loss (0.030) is worse than nearest-neighbor retrieval (0.021).\n- The method is specific to Blender, and the authors note that inference-time syntax checking and a compact DSL are future work."}]},"uuid":"be73ad7f-82b7-45e7-97b5-7fa53f242bf4","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:47.199Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"294b8496-94c3-4b16-8dee-f04c1fae6064","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3917a182-2a1d-4b2f-a630-43f5e22b0c0c","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
