{"parentUuid":"992e59ac-2584-4237-9af1-9fdcee9d79e7","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SvS5LN8ohLUqpJLSWUagzQ","type":"tool_result","content":"The page doesn't include what you're asking for. It's a publication index that lists titles, authors, venues, and links, but no abstracts or method descriptions. The policy, reward, algorithm, training data, and results aren't described anywhere in the visible content, and the listing was truncated.\n\nThe likely match is **\"Procedural Material Generation with Reinforcement Learning\"** by B. Li, Y. Hu, P. Guerrero, M. Hasan, L. Shi, V. Deschaintre, and W. Matusik. It appeared in *ACM Transactions on Graphics* 43(6), 2024 (DOI: 10.1145/3687979). The page links to its code and PDF, which should have the abstract and method details. The listed code is at github.com/adobe-research/ProcMatRL.\n\n[WebFetch note: this page's text is 309438 characters long and the answer above covers only characters 0 to 100000; the final 209438 were not read — to read on, call WebFetch again with the same url and offset: 100000.]"}]},"uuid":"df6a1ebc-0d85-40a0-b4eb-8d0227548c83","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:23:59.145Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8d9a28f0-b0d5-4c8b-9b8b-d2e4735300a0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"992e59ac-2584-4237-9af1-9fdcee9d79e7","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"e9c11242-38b1-4a60-99f3-8b40d926382f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014myqvtkDKQdZcynoFkLe7i","type":"tool_result","content":"**1. Graph representation**\n- Materials are directed acyclic graphs, stored as a compact, YAML-based text format called CompactSBS, with nodes in topological order. The paper says \"Models operate exclusively in CompactSBS, with outputs transpiled back to SBS for execution.\"\n- Visual input is a rendered preview of each node's intermediate output. *Mixed* conditioning adds a per-node image field to the text. *Graph* conditioning uses one visualization of the whole graph with embedded outputs, and no text node definitions.\n\n**2. Model and training data**\n- Base model: Qwen2.5-VL (7B).\n- Data: over 6,000 unique materials from Adobe's Substance 3D Assets Repository, targeting Substance Designer. Graphs with embedded bitmaps or SVGs were excluded, as were graphs over 128 nodes. Substance 3D Community Assets were excluded for licensing reasons.\n- Training: 5 epochs, AdamW, learning rate 5e-5, batch size 128, maximum sequence length 8,192 tokens.\n\n**3. Tree search inference**\n- Expansion: the model generates one node at a time. Each node is transpiled and executed, and its output is rendered and fed back as context.\n- Pruning/backtracking: a failed node is discarded and resampled. Repeated failures trigger backtracking that discards 2^(i-1) recent nodes at iteration i.\n- Scoring: the paper describes no value function. Validity (transpilation and execution success) is the only filter. Sampling uses temperature 0.8 and top-p 0.95. For human evaluation, 40 candidates were sampled per model and image, and the one with the highest DreamSim was kept.\n- Visual feedback: rendered intermediate node outputs.\n- Error repair: removes parameters unsupported by a node type and inserts grayscale or gradient-map conversion nodes for type mismatches.\n\n**4. Evaluation metrics**\n- Unconditional: KID (visual quality), ROUGE-L (memorization), and Node Error Ratio (NER, efficiency).\n- Conditional: DreamSim, CLIP similarity, Style Loss, plus KID, ROUGE-L, and NER.\n- Human: comparative annotation by eight experts on 33 challenging inputs.\n\n**5. Results (scores ×100)**\n- Unconditional (KID / ROUGE-L / NER): VLMaterial (SBS) 14.155 / 3.641 / 14.846; MultiMat (Mixed) 6.752 / 2.195 / 8.923; MultiMat (Graph) 2.365 / 1.915 / 15.024.\n- Conditional, without optimization (DSim / CLIP / KID / NER): VLMaterial (SBS) 31.344 / 65.678 / 14.976 / 16.933; MultiMat (Mixed) 34.922 / 66.737 / 3.675 / 12.388; MultiMat (Graph) 36.609 / 67.907 / 2.801 / 17.046.\n- Conditional, with parameter optimization: MultiMat+ (Graph) reaches DSim 40.367 and CLIP 70.114, versus 40.258 and 69.687 for MultiMat+ (Mixed). VLMaterial+ (SBS) reaches DSim 31.348.\n- Human preference: MultiMat+ (Graph) was most preferred and VLMaterial+ (SBS) least preferred.\n- The paper also reports that disabling tree search raises VLMaterial (SBS)'s NER from 14.846 to 33.953.\n\n**6. Limitations**\n- Training is much slower: MultiMat takes several days on 8×A100 80GB GPUs, versus a few hours for VLMaterial, because it trains one node at a time. Inference throughput is similar.\n- MultiMat (Graph) has the highest NER in the unconditional setting. The authors attribute its errors mainly to OCR-like misreading of node names and function types in graph images.\n- Parameter optimization improves perceptual similarity but raises KID. The authors suggest this reflects alignment with the test subset.\n- Failure cases remain on intricate patterns, and the training data is small."}]},"uuid":"67cd3a39-5a4c-47c5-b911-3aaffc2b6b18","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:24:07.419Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"8d9a28f0-b0d5-4c8b-9b8b-d2e4735300a0","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branc