{"parentUuid":"718e34fa-b0f3-456b-93ba-74f3522bf9f4","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EdYd1XqbUoEgiQgnyZE73w","type":"tool_result","content":"**Paper:** *Procedural Material Generation with Reinforcement Learning* (SIGGRAPH Asia 2024), by Beichen Li, Yiwei Hu, Paul Guerrero, Milos Hasan, Liang Shi, Valentin Deschaintre, and Wojciech Matusik.\n\n**Abstract summary:**\n\n- **Problem:** Procedural materials are widely used in 3D content creation but are hard to manipulate. A prior method (Hu et al. 2023) generates procedural graphs that match an input image, but its parameter prediction is often inaccurate because it relies on supervised training with little procedural data.\n- **Proposal:** The authors present the first reinforcement learning (RL) approach for procedural materials to improve parameter prediction. RL avoids three obstacles: the scarcity of procedural training data, the gap between real and synthetic materials, and the need for end-to-end differentiable loss functions.\n- **Method:** Given a target image, the system retrieves a procedural material. An RL-trained transformer then predicts a set of parameters that reconstruct the target image as closely as possible.\n- **Results:** RL-based parameter prediction matches target images better than supervised methods, on both synthetic and real targets.\n\n**RL approach and reward:** The abstract does not describe the reward function, the RL algorithm, the state and action spaces, or the training setup. The full paper would be needed for those details."}]},"uuid":"affa981f-cdc7-48dc-85c0-c7abbeef552f","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:24:17.999Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"ae7ac167-f704-4742-879b-e660b89b0c7a","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"}