{"parentUuid":"3e68881a-01a3-4540-8a7d-df9e6734ccc2","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014pjFeYHrTMeY9qMrd9v29D","type":"tool_result","content":"# Pipeline Summary: \"An Inverse Procedural Modeling Pipeline for SVBRDF Maps\"\n\n**Inputs:** Albedo, normal, and roughness maps. Normal maps are converted to height maps via Poisson reconstruction so they are easier to proceduralize.\n\n## 1. Decomposition (structure vs. appearance)\n\n- **Hierarchical tree:** The material is split iteratively into a tree of sub-materials. Each node's mask encodes where a sub-material sits, and each leaf's maps encode its local appearance.\n- **Spectrum-aware KNN matting:** The user draws a few scribbles to mark regions. Affinity features include albedo RGB, height, roughness, position, and a local noise-spectrum feature (Welch's method, reduced to 3 dimensions with PCA). Alpha maps are thresholded into binary masks.\n- **Instance-based decomposition:** For repeating patterns such as tiles, instances are extracted from a mask, scaled to a common size by bounding box, described by color histograms and local spectra, and grouped with agglomerative clustering.\n\n## 2. Procedural mask models\n\n- **PPTBF by example:** Point Process Texture Basis Functions are fit to binary masks in two steps:\n  - *Query:* Nearest-neighbor retrieval from a ~450K-image database, using LBP, VGG19 Gram-matrix, and Fourier power-spectrum features, reduced to 512 dimensions.\n  - *Optimization:* Coordinate descent alternates Powell's method (continuous parameters) and Bayesian optimization (discrete parameters).\n- **Incomplete lower-level masks:** Losses are computed only on unmasked pixels, or the sub-material is inpainted with PatchMatch and segmented afterward.\n- **Random sampling:** Instance-based masks are regenerated by assigning labels according to estimated occurrence frequencies.\n\n## 3. Appearance (noise and statistics)\n\n- **Multi-layer noise model:** The image is progressively Gaussian-filtered with growing kernel sizes. Each residual layer is fit with random-phase noise using sliding-window spectrum estimation. The mean of the lowest-frequency layer becomes the base color.\n- **Masked regions:** Missing data is inpainted (Telea and PatchMatch), and a Gabor-noise basis approximates the power spectrum of the inpainted result.\n- **Multichannel handling:** Synthesis happens in PCA color space, followed by histogram matching to restore the input color distribution.\n\n## 4. Losses and recomposition\n\n- **Optimizable operators:** Each noise map is modified by a Gaussian filter with intensity, bias, and blur parameters. Masks are smoothed with a Gaussian blur. Noise is multiplied by masks and combined bottom-up.\n- **Differentiable rendering:** Input and procedural maps are rendered with a Cook-Torrance GGX model under random lighting.\n- **Loss:** A style term (L1 difference of VGG Gram matrices of renderings) plus a weighted SSIM term (L1 difference of SSIM indices).\n- **Optimizer:** L-BFGS-B, learning rate 0.005, about 200 steps.\n\n## 5. Results\n\n- Outputs are fully procedural albedo, normal, and roughness maps that reproduce both large-scale structure and fine detail.\n- Results can be rendered at arbitrary resolution (e.g., 2048×1024) and super-resolved by upsampling masks only, without changing scale.\n- Parameters allow editing of masks, noise, and operators.\n- The pipeline outperforms or complements Hu et al. (2019) and MATch (Shi et al., 2020) without requiring pre-existing material graphs. It also compares favorably to example-based texture synthesis methods, which lack editability.\n- Typical cost is under 5 minutes of user interaction and about 20 minutes of compute, though the implementation is not optimized.\n\n## 6. Limitations\n\n- **Taxonomy:** The authors define four axes of complexity: number of element types, density and overlap of elements, mask contour complexity, and structure within elements. The current system only handles the simpler end of the last three.\n- **Dense or overlapping elements:** Densely packed sub-materials, such as small pebbles, are not segmented or reproduced well.\n- **Complex contours and semantic shapes:** PPTBF masks lose details such as semantically meaningful shapes (e.g., a flower) and can drift in contour detail.\n- **Highly structured normals:** Gaussian-based noise models struggle with strong directional normal variation, and height segmentation can be ambiguous.\n- **User dependence:** Segmentation relies on user scribbles, and the authors note that current automatic segmentation methods fail on these materials.\n\n## Future directions (as stated by the authors)\n\n- Deep generative texture models for complex appearances\n- Fully automatic segmentation\n- More advanced differentiable filters and generators\n\n[WebFetch note: this page's text is 118902 characters long and the answer above covers only characters 0 to 100000; the final 18902 were not read — to read on, call WebFetch again with the same url and offset: 100000.]"}]},"uuid":"70eb003a-907d-4ff1-9289-46250b5f9b7e","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:24:39.861Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"5b31a403-72fc-4296-87ca-2a3975035c14","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"3e68881a-01a3-4540-8a7d-df9e6734ccc2","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
