or tailored image texture in iterative image reconstruction\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/11270477\"},{\"title\":\"Image blending using linear blends of corresponding neighborhood means and variances from first and second image regions\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/7454087\"},{\"title\":\"NAN: Noise-Aware NeRFs for Burst-Denoising\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2204.04668\"},{\"title\":\"Locally adapted histogram equalization\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/6650774\"},{\"title\":\"Image blending using non-affine interpolation\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/7512288\"},{\"title\":\"Noisy histograms\",\"url\":\"https://image-ppubs.uspto.gov/dirsearch-public/print/downloadPdf/7363192\"},{\"title\":\"patents.google.com\",\"url\":\"https://patents.google.com/patent/US7454087\"}]\n\nI found the paper's bibliographic record, though the search didn't return the full text or the publisher's page.\n\n**Bibliographic details:** The paper is \"High-Performance By-Example Noise using a Histogram-Preserving Blending Operator\" by Eric Heitz (Unity Technologies) and Fabrice Neyret (CNRS/LJK, Inria). It was published online on August 24, 2018, with DOI 10.1145/3233304 and 25 pages. The Crossref metadata lists it as volume 1, which fits the HPG 2018 issue of the ACM journal, though the search results don't name the journal.\n\n**Content (from the abstract as indexed by Crossref):**\n- The authors analyze problems that arise with linear blending by examining its effect on the histogram. They argue that a blending operator which preserves the histogram avoids those problems.\n- Their target is a per-pixel operator that can run in a fragment shader without post-processing.\n- For Gaussian inputs, histogram-preservation reduces to keeping the mean and variance, which can be computed analytically.\n- For non-Gaussian inputs, they transform the data toward a Gaussian distribution before blending, then apply the inverse transform afterward.\n- These histogram transformations are precomputed and stored so the method can run in a shader.\n\nI didn't find the full text, the ACM Digital Library page, or the project page in the results. If you need the exact figures, the paper's equations, or the journal name, check the DOI link.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"c43d3e74-2dba-451d-896e-49d63c81ff4d","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:26:05.919Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"05001b92-fac9-441b-8c1c-7d0e1a480149","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"f59ff561-ce72-47a8-a19b-9f023e09fd6f","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
