{"parentUuid":"d8a3daa4-61a6-410e-9d64-50e7b2e13d9d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018TSFswfrjLQ4553bWSui4j","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Heitz Vanhoey Chambon Belcour \"A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis\" CVPR 2021\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.07229\"},{\"title\":\"A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/html/Heitz_A_Sliced_Wasserstein_Loss_for_Neural_Texture_Synthesis_CVPR_2021_paper.html\"},{\"title\":\"Heitz A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis CVPR 2021 paper\",\"url\":\"https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2021/papers/Heitz_A_Sliced_Wasserstein_Loss_for_Neural_Texture_Synthesis_CVPR_2021_paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.07229v4\"},{\"title\":\"A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://ar5iv.arxiv.org/html/2006.07229\"},{\"title\":\"A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://mlanthology.org/cvpr/2021/heitz2021cvpr-sliced\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2006.07229v4\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.11137?context=cs\"},{\"title\":\"License: CC BY 4.0\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2211.11137v2\"}]\n\nThe paper is by Eric Heitz, Kenneth Vanhoey, Thomas Chambon, and Laurent Belcour of Unity Technologies. It appeared at CVPR 2021, and the proceedings list it on pages 9412-9420. doi:10.1109/CVPR46437.2021.00929\n\nThe authors argue that the standard Gram-matrix loss used in neural texture synthesis has drawbacks, and they propose the Sliced Wasserstein Distance as an alternative. Their abstract says the method is theoretically proven, practical, simple to implement, and achieves results that are visually superior for texture synthesis by optimization or training generative neural networks.\n\nThe paper is also on arXiv as 2006.07229. The arXiv listing notes it was accepted at CVPR 2021.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"d4cfc3a7-9040-4f74-a2e8-916073dbfcbf","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:26:16.100Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"50c8a38f-dfd3-42ac-a521-5be42f0b6dce","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"d8a3daa4-61a6-410e-9d64-50e7b2e13d9d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"762c1e68-96ff-44fa-8c81-41afc4525f92","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AQRaQkRVuuHpKHu3QV6aMz","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"DISTS \"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\" Ding Ma Wang Simoncelli texture resampling tolerance\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.07728\"},{\"title\":\"Image quality assessment: Unifying structure and texture similarity\",\"url\":\"https://www.cns.nyu.edu/~lcv/pubs/makeAbs.php?loc=Ding20\"},{\"title\":\"image quality assessment unifying structure and texture similarit\",\"url\":\"https://rims.cityu-dg.edu.cn/en/publications/image-quality-assessment-unifying-structure-and-texture-similarit/\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.07728v2\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2004.07728\"},{\"title\":\"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\",\"url\":\"https://deepai.org/publication/image-quality-assessment-unifying-structure-and-texture-similarity\"},{\"title\":\"hyper.ai\",\"url\":\"https://hyper.ai/en/papers/2004.07728\"},{\"title\":\"image quality assessment unifying structure and texture similarit\",\"url\":\"https://scholars.cityu.edu.hk/en/publications/image-quality-assessment-unifying-structure-and-texture-similarit/\"},{\"title\":\"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\",\"url\":\"https://fugumt.com/fugumt/paper_check/2004.07728v3_enmode\"}]\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.07728\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2004.07728v2\"},{\"title\":\"image quality assessment unifying structure and texture similarit\",\"url\":\"https://scholars.cityu.edu.hk/en/publications/image-quality-assessment-unifying-structure-and-texture-similarit/\"},{\"title\":\"image quality assessment unifying structure and texture similarit\",\"url\":\"https://rims.cityu-dg.edu.cn/en/publications/image-quality-assessment-unifying-structure-and-texture-similarit/\"},{\"title\":\"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\",\"url\":\"https://deepai.org/publication/image-quality-assessment-unifying-structure-and-texture-similarity\"},{\"title\":\"Keyan Ding, Kede Ma, Shiqi Wang, Eero P. Simoncelli\",\"url\":\"https://papercopilot.com/cat/cs/page/15/\"},{\"title\":\"hyper.ai\",\"url\":\"https://hyper.ai/en/papers/2004.07728\"},{\"title\":\"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity\",\"url\":\"https://fugumt.com/fugumt/paper_check/2004.07728v3_enmode\"},{\"title\":\"Deep Image Structure And Texture Similarity (DISTS)\",\"url\":\"https://lightning.ai/docs/torchmetrics/stable/image/dists.html\"},{\"title\":\"Share your thoughts, 1 month free Claude Pro on usSee more\",\"url\":\"https://www.wizwand.com/paper/68fa69161e209131cea201e4\"}]\n\nThe DISTS paper is Keyan Ding, Kede Ma, Shiqi Wang, and Eero P. Simoncelli's \"Image Quality Assessment: Unifying Structure and Texture Similarity.\" It was published in IEEE TPAMI, vol. 44, no. 5, pp. 2567–2581. The NYU listing dates it to December 2020, while the CityU record gives the print date as May 2022.\n\n**Texture resampling tolerance**\nThe core motivation is that standard full-reference metrics treat texture swaps badly. Relative to human observers, these measures are overly sensitive to resampling of texture regions (eg, replacing one patch of grass with another). The authors present DISTS as the first full-reference image quality model with explicit tolerance to texture resampling.\n\nTheir Figure 1 illustrates the problem. A JPEG-compressed grass image was compared against a resampled grass image. PSNR, SSIM, FSIM, VIF, GMSD, DeepIQA, PieAPP, and LPIPS all ranked the compressed image as higher quality, which conflicts with human ratings. DISTS made the human-consistent prediction.\n\n**Method**\n- A CNN builds an injective and differentiable function that transforms images to multi-scale overcomplete representations.\n- Spatial averages of the feature maps capture texture appearance. The authors show they provide a set of sufficient statistical constraints to synthesize a wide variety of texture patterns.\n- The final score combines correlations of those spatial averages (\"texture similarity\") with correlations of the feature maps (\"structure similarity\").\n- The parameters were tuned to match human quality ratings while also minimizing the reported distances between subimages cropped from the same texture images.\n- The network is based on pre-trained VGG, and the authors describe the spatial averages as a parametric texture model similar to Portilla & Simoncelli's.\n\n**Other results**\n- The paper reports that the method explains human perceptual scores, both on conventional image quality databases, as well as on texture databases.\n- It also reports competitive performance on related tasks such as texture classification and retrieval.\n- It is relatively insensitive to geometric transformations (\\eg, translation and dilation), without use of any specialized training or data augmentation.\n\n**Limitations**\nThe authors note that DISTS enjoys several advantageous mathematical properties, it is still highly non-convex (with abundant saddle points and plateaus), which makes optimization slower than with SSIM.\n\n**Implementation**\nThe official code is at github.com/dingkeyan93/DISTS. TorchMetrics also includes it as Deep Image Structure And Texture Similarity (DISTS).\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"890320ee-91ba-42c1-b6c3-de67bc955c1c","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14: