{"parentUuid":"721e5e29-0006-46c0-ba76-25df7318a4e8","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BfN5Es3wqyWxn4vR8XMFhS","type":"tool_result","content":"**Histogram COP** builds a histogram from a layer to show the distribution of its values.\n\n**Inputs**\n- **source**: The layer or VDB to analyze.\n- **active**: An optional layer or VDB that limits which pixels count. Only pixels where it is at least `0.5` contribute.\n\n**Outputs**\n- **histogram**: A visualization with the same type and size as `source`. Its appearance depends on the Mode.\n- **counts**: A layer of raw bucket values. Its width equals the bucket count and its height is `5`. Rows 1–4 (from the bottom) hold the red, green, blue, and alpha counts. The fifth row stores the total processed pixel count in column 0. The page does not say whether the counts are cumulative.\n\n**Parameters**\n- **Mode**: Colored Bars (a horizontal ramp colored by pixel count), Separate Bars (grayscale bars per channel), or Graph (bar height per channel, with channels overlapping).\n- **Buckets**: The number of intervals the range is divided into. Its role is the same as a bin count.\n- **Minimum / Maximum**: The range to bucket. Typical values are `0` and `1`. The dropper can set them from the layer.\n- **Outside Values**: *Discard* ignores out-of-range values. *Clamp* adds them to the first or last bucket.\n- **Scale**: Scales the bar heights to improve legibility.\n- **Channel Scope**: Selects which of R, G, B, and A the node affects.\n\nThe page does not list a cumulative parameter.\n\n**Histogram matching**\nThe page does not describe histogram matching. It offers two relevant pieces:\n- The **counts** output exposes the raw per-channel distribution, which you could compare between two layers using an external process.\n- The page points to **Remap** for shifting the range of a grayscale image, but it does not say how to match one layer's distribution to another's."}]},"uuid":"80cbdcf0-67e0-42d3-a01e-42c9c2d8d8f2","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:29:35.603Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"563a85d2-3089-4463-af80-ccfb98ae01b1","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":829,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":5},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"721e5e29-0006-46c0-ba76-25df7318a4e8","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"80543682-47e5-47ac-b33f-d402439d0521","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01PHc63uKhraHnxBBgs48Ljc","type":"tool_result","content":"The Python Snippet COP runs Python code on layers and geometry inside Copernicus. The page doesn't say how layer data is represented (for example, whether layers arrive as numpy arrays), so I can't confirm that.\n\n**Layer access**\n- The code gets a `kwargs` dictionary containing the bound parameters and the input geometry and layers.\n- Bound parameters are fixed constants evaluated before the code runs. Because they live in `kwargs`, the code runs independently of any particular node.\n- Geometry can't be bound as a layer. You refer to it by name in an attribute or volume binding.\n\n**Inputs and outputs**\n- The **Signature** tab defines inputs and outputs. Each has a name, a type, and for inputs an Optional flag.\n- Layer types include Mono, UV, RGB, RGBA, ID, and Varying (which matches the other Varying ports). Other types include Geometry, Metadata (used only for size and metadata), and integer, float, and vector VDBs.\n- The code returns a dictionary of the named outputs.\n- Bindings (in the **Bindings** tab) can be Integer, Float, Vector2, Vector, Vector4, String, or Ramp. Ramps become `hou.Ramp` objects.\n- Unlike the Python Script SOP, this node can't access the node that is currently evaluating.\n\n**Performance**\n- Python is CPU-based and interpreted, so per-pixel operations are slow. The page recommends OpenCL or VEX for those.\n- The **Maintain State** option keeps the interpreter between cooks. This can improve performance but may cause unexpected behavior, so the page recommends leaving it off in most cases.\n\n**Statistics and writing results**\n- The page doesn't say whether the node has built-in statistics functions. Since the code is ordinary Python, you could compute