{"parentUuid":"20dcf52e-5cd5-4e8e-8515-8052000877d0","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"f51e2112-0260-44ed-9b89-c69d1eac8b4d","agentId":"ae649ce7dcad97197","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01JqNpC4KGg9XQvY4xSC1xJb","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Galerne Gousseau Morel \"Random Phase Textures: Theory and Synthesis\" IEEE TIP 2011 asymptotic discrete spot noise\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Random phase textures: Theory and synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://researchportal.ip-paris.fr/en/publications/random-phase-textures-theory-and-synthesis/\"},{\"title\":\"ipol.im\",\"url\":\"https://ipol.im/pub/art/2011/ggm_rpn/revisions/2011-09-23/article.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"itts023d.itts.ttu.edu\",\"url\":\"https://itts023d.itts.ttu.edu/ordb/Data/146975\"},{\"title\":\"itts023d.itts.ttu.edu\",\"url\":\"https://itts023d.itts.ttu.edu/_site/Data/146975/?db=2\"},{\"title\":\"random phase textures theory and synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://scholars.cityu.edu.hk/en/publications/random-phase-textures-theory-and-synthesis/\"},{\"title\":\"Micro-Texture Synthesis by Phase Randomization\",\"url\":\"https://ipol.im/pub/art/2011/ggm_rpn/revisions/2011-09-23/ggm_rpn.html\"},{\"title\":\"itts023d.itts.ttu.edu\",\"url\":\"https://itts023d.itts.ttu.edu/ORModelDB/Data/146975\"},{\"title\":\"random phase textures theory and synthesis\",\"url\":\"https://scholars.ln.edu.hk/en/publications/random-phase-textures-theory-and-synthesis/\"},{\"title\":\"itts023d.itts.ttu.edu\",\"url\":\"https://itts023d.itts.ttu.edu/OdorMapDB/Data/146975\"},{\"title\":\"ipol.im\",\"url\":\"https://ipol.im/pub/art/2011/ggm_rpn/article.pdf\"}]\n\nThe paper is by Bruno Galerne, Yann Gousseau, and Jean-Michel Morel, published in IEEE Transactions on Image Processing, volume 20, issue 1, pages 257–267 (2011). The DOI is 10.1109/TIP.2010.2052822.\n\n**What the paper covers:** The authors analyze two sample-based texture models, random phase noise (RPN) and asymptotic discrete spot noise (ADSN). Their central finding is that the two are mathematically distinct stochastic processes, even though the results look alike. The mathematical analysis shows that, contrary to some statements in the literature, RPN and ADSN are different stochastic processes. Still, experiments suggest that textures generated from identical samples by these algorithms are perceptually similar. The models are also described as linearized versions of two early Julesz texture discrimination theories.\n\n**Technical contributions:** The paper adds three extensions. It extends the RPN and ADSN algorithms to color images. It also proposes a preprocessing step to avoid artifacts caused by the nonperiodicity of real-world texture samples. Finally, the method is extended to synthesize textures with arbitrary size from a given sample.\n\n**Companion work:** The same authors published a demo paper in Image Processing On Line in 2011 (vol. 1, pp. 213–237), \"Micro-Texture Synthesis by Phase Randomization,\" which synthesizes a texture from an original image by randomizing its Fourier phase.\n\nNote that the search results contained one citation database entry listing the last page as \"67\" instead of 267. The page range 257–267 is consistent across the publisher and institutional records, so 267 is the likely correct value.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"98cc23f4-7a7e-45b1-bdb6-07ca47071aa4","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:39:49.288Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"7885e881-de74-49af-a6ee-c7f96a2a3dad","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":null,"branch":null,"default_branch":null,"status":null,"visibility":null,"error":"pending"},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"20dcf52e-5cd5-4e8e-8515-8052000877d0","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userTyp