,\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1505.07376\"},{\"title\":\"Inferring low-dimensional microstructure representations using convolutional neural networks\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1611.02764\"},{\"title\":\"a5e00132373a7031000fd987a3c9f87b Paper\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/file/a5e00132373a7031000fd987a3c9f87b-Paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"a5e00132373a7031000fd987a3c9f87b Paper\",\"url\":\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2015/file/a5e00132373a7031000fd987a3c9f87b-Paper.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Texture Synthesis Using Convolutional Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://alphaxiv.org/abs/1505.07376\"},{\"title\":\"Texture Synthesis Using Convolutional Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://papers.nips.cc/paper/2015/hash/a5e00132373a7031000fd987a3c9f87b-Abstract.html\"},{\"title\":\"Texture synthesis using convolutional neural networks\",\"url\":\"https://bethgelab.org/publication/2015_01_gatys/\"},{\"title\":\"Texture Synthesis Using Convolutional Neural Networks\",\"url\":\"https://deepai.org/publication/texture-synthesis-using-convolutional-neural-networks\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1606.00021\"},{\"title\":\"Share your thoughts, 1 month free Claude Pro on usSee more\",\"url\":\"https://www.wizwand.com/paper/68fa5ec31e209131ce9e5065\"}]\n\n**Heeger & Bergen (1995), \"Pyramid-Based Texture Analysis/Synthesis\"**\n\nThe method starts with an input digitized texture and a noise image, typically uniform white noise. It modifies the noise so it looks like the input texture. It uses an invertible image representation known as an image pyramid, along with a function, match-histogram, that matches the histograms of two images. The authors presented examples using two pyramids: the Laplacian pyramid (a radially symmetric transform) and the steerable pyramid (an oriented transform).\n\nThe core loop alternates between two domains. Starting from a white noise image, histogram matchings are performed alternately in the image domain and the steerable pyramid domain, so that the corresponding histograms match those of the input texture. On iterations, a later analysis notes that the method works well for stochastic textures and empirically converges after few iterations.\n\n**Gatys, Ecker & Bethge (2015), \"Texture Synthesis Using Convolutional Neural Networks\" (arXiv 1505.07376)**\n\nThe paper was published at NIPS 2015, and its authors are Leon A. Gatys, Alexander S. Ecker, and Matthias Bethge. Its model represents textures by the correlations between feature maps in several layers of the network.\n\nSynthesis is an optimization problem. Starting from a white noise image, gradient descent finds another image that matches the Gram-matrix representation of the original image. The loss minimizes the mean-squared distance between the entries of the Gram matrix of the original image and that of the generated image. The Gram matrix at layer l is defined as the correlations between feature i and feature k, summed over pixel positions.\n\n**How the two compare**\n\nBoth methods start from white noise and iteratively reshape it to match statistics of an exemplar. Heeger–Bergen matches per-subband histograms, which are first-order statistics, using a hand-built steerable pyramid. Gatys et al. match second-order correlations (Gram matrices) of CNN features learned for object recognition, and they optimize a loss by gradient descent rather than alternating exact histogram matching. The Gatys paper's own abstract notes that across layers the texture representations increasingly capture the statistical properties of natural images while making object information more and more explicit.\n\nNote that the arXiv listing dates vary across aggregators. One lists 05/27/2015, and another lists 2015-11-06, which likely reflects a later revision. I did not verify which version is canonical, so check the arXiv page directly if the exact date matters.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"uuid":"904f1217-6b01-4625-9b8f-c6777dcd0d40","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:40:06.225Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"d63db536-ae1f-4674-9dca-209c4106e0fe","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","root":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","branch":"main","default_branch":"main","status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":27,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":0},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"matthieuhuguet/Memory","visibility":"private"}]}},"live_cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","platform":"macos"}},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c6b2043c-ffbc-4bda-a033-abca67a66191","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"user_permanent","reasonType":"rule"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
