every output patch has a close patch in the target and every target patch has a close patch in the output. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): for every 16 by 16 patch of the current output, find the nearest reference patch, and the reverse; reference patches with no close match form clusters that name the missing feature (for example every patch containing a chip edge); the largest cluster by area becomes the next layer, and the window size at which matches turn poor gives the scale of that feature. (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.2 Image quilting and texture transfer, Efros and Freeman 2001 (11 October 2026)\n\n- Reference: A. A. Efros, W. T. Freeman, \"Image Quilting for Texture Synthesis and Transfer\", SIGGRAPH 2001, https://people.eecs.berkeley.edu/~efros/research/quilting.html (course material and summaries read). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: overlapping blocks of the sample are laid down, each chosen among blocks that agree with the existing overlap, and a minimum error boundary cut computed by dynamic programming stitches it in; texture transfer adds a correspondence term so that blocks follow a guide image. (11 October 2026)\n- Known failures: the \"garbage growing\" and verbatim copying of pixel methods survive, only at block scale (analysis in https://ipol.im/pub/art/2017/171/article_lr.pdf, read through a summary). (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): transfer is the semi procedural answer for detail that no noise reproduces; a guide built from our procedural structure (tile ID, distance to grout, line mask) selects reference blocks for the micro detail, so detail lands where the structure says it should; to be used only when borrowing reference statistics is acceptable for the project (method M14). (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.3 Wang tiles, Cohen, Shade, Hiller and Deussen 2003 (11 October 2026)\n\n- Reference: M. F. Cohen, J. Shade, S. Hiller, O. Deussen, \"Wang Tiles for Image and Texture Generation\", ACM Transactions on Graphics 22(3), SIGGRAPH 2003, pp. 287 to 294, https://graphics.uni-konstanz.de/publikationen/Cohen2003WangTilesImage/index.html (abstract read). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: square tiles carry colored edges, neighbors must share an edge color, and a stochastic runtime algorithm tiles the plane non periodically from a small set; tiles may carry texture, Poisson point sets or geometry, and a corner coding lets objects straddle edges. (11 October 2026)\n- Statistic it implies: no long range period while every local configuration stays valid; unwanted repetition appears as sharp peaks in the power spectrum and in the autocorrelation. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): when the reference residual has no periodic peak but our output does (a noise or exemplar repeating with the tile grid), the next move is a de-periodization layer, per tile random offsets read from the tile ID (`randommono` keyed on the `tilepattern` id output) or the `hextile` COP; success is the disappearance of the spurious spectral peaks. (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.4 Histogram preserving blending, Heitz and Neyret 2018 (11 October 2026)\n\n- Reference: E. Heitz, F. Neyret, \"High-Performance By-Example Noise using a Histogram-Preserving Blending Operator\", HPG 2018 journal track, volume 1, DOI 10.1145/3233304 (abstract read through Crossref). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: linear blending of several samples of a texture alters its histogram, shrinking variance and washing out contrast; a histogram preserving operator avoids it; for Gaussian inputs keeping mean and variance is enough and is analytic (blend weights renormalized by the square root of the sum of squared weights); non Gaussian inputs are first mapped to a Gaussian by a precomputed transform, blended, then mapped back, all per pixel in a shader. (11 October 2026)\n- Statistic it implies: every blend must preserve the first order histogram of heights. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): before adding a noise layer, compare the variance of the output residual with the variance of the reference residual in the same region; if the output is flatter, the defect is a contrast killing blend rather than a missing layer, and the fix is a variance preserving blend or a final quantile remap (`remap` with a measured ramp); in Copernicus the Gaussianize, blend and inverse chain fits in one `wrangle` fed with transfer curves sampled from the target CDF (UNVERIFIED in practice). (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.5 Gabor noise, by example noise and random phase noise (11 October 2026)\n\n- References: A. Lagae, S. Lefebvre, G. Drettakis, P. Dutré, \"Procedural noise using sparse Gabor convolution\", ACM Transactions on Graphics 28(3), SIGGRAPH 2009; B. Galerne, A. Lagae, S. Lefebvre, G. Drettakis, \"Gabor Noise by Example\", ACM Transactions on Graphics 31(4), article 73, SIGGRAPH 2012, https://hal-univ-tlse3.archives-ouvertes.fr/INRIA/hal-00695670; B. Galerne, Y. Gousseau, J. M. Morel, \"Random Phase Textures: Theory and Synthesis\", IEEE Transactions on Image Processing 20(1), 2011, pp. 257 to 267, demo https://ipol.im/pub/art/2011/ggm_rpn/article.pdf (abstracts read). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: a Gaussian texture is entirely described by its power spectrum; Gabor noise by example decomposes the noisy spectrum estimate of an exemplar into a sparse sum of Gaussians by non negative basis pursuit denoising, then evaluates the noise procedurally with multi grid evaluation and importance sampling of kernel parameters; random phase noise keeps the Fourier modulus of the sample and draws a uniform random phase, after a preprocessing step that removes the artifacts of non periodic samples. (11 October 2026)\n- Statistic it implies: the full 2D power spectrum (radial and angular) plus the mean; nothing about phase alignment, so edges, cracks and chips are out of reach of this family. (11 October 2026)\n- COP mapping: Copernicus offers `fractalnoise`, `phasornoise`, `worleynoise`, `cellularnoise`, `crystalnoise`, and since Houdini 22 `filterfrequencies`, whose optional `filter` input is used directly as the frequency domain mask, plus `fft` and `convolvefilter` (FFT convolution); white noise from `randommono` filtered by a measured amplitude mask is therefore a random phase noise built inside Copernicus (PROPOSED; the expected layout of the filter image, centered or corner origin, is UNVERIFIED). (11 October 2026)\n- Phasor noise: T. Tricard, S. Efremov, C. Zanni, F. Neyret, J. Martínez, S. Lefebvre, \"Procedural Phasor Noise\", ACM Transactions on Graphics 38(4), SIGGRAPH 2019 (recalled reference, not re-read today); it separates the oscillation phase from the amplitude to produce contrasted, controllable wave patterns, available as `phasornoise` since Houdini 21. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): the band of the radially averaged spectrum where the log ratio between reference residual and output residual is most negative is the missing scale; the angular sector with the largest deficit is the missing orientation; each Gaussian lobe of a sparse spectral fit equals one candidate noise layer (center frequency, bandwidth, orientation), so the fit lists the next noise layers in decreasing energy. (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.6 Pyramid histogram matching, Heeger and Bergen 1995 (11 October 2026)\n\n- Reference: D. J. Heeger, J. R. Bergen, \"Pyramid-Based Texture Analysis/Synthesis\", SIGGRAPH 1995, https://inc.ucsd.edu/~marni/Igert/Heeger_1995.pdf, and the IPOL reimplementation https://www.ipol.im/pub/art/2014/79/article_lr.pdf (summaries read). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: starting from white noise, histograms are matched alternately in the image domain and in every subband of a pyramid (Laplacian or steerable), a few iterations being enough for stochastic textures; Lefebvre and Poulin used it to fill tile interiors (section 1.2). (11 October 2026)\n- Statistic it implies: the marginal histogram of the image and of each subband, per scale and orientation. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): compare band by band the histogram of the reference residual with that of the output (Wasserstein 1D, in height units); the band with the largest distance hosts the next layer, and the skewness of the reference band gives the sign of the needed feature (negative skew in a fine band means missing narrow pits or cracks, positive skew means missing narrow ridges such as raised motif lines). (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.7 Joint wavelet statistics, Portilla and Simoncelli 2000 (11 October 2026)\n\n- Reference: J. Portilla, E. P. Simoncelli, \"A Parametric Texture Model Based on Joint Statistics of Complex Wavelet Coefficients\", International Journal of Computer Vision 40(1), 2000, https://www.cns.nyu.edu/pub/eero/portilla99-reprint.pdf, code https://github.com/LabForComputationalVision/textureSynth and the PyTorch port in plenoptic (abstract and summaries read). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: a texture is described by statistics of a complex steerable pyramid, namely the first moments of the pixel histogram, the local autocorrelation of coefficients in each subband, the auto and cross correlations of coefficient magnitudes across neighboring positions, orientations and scales, and a cross scale phase statistic (fine coefficients against phase doubled coarse coefficients); synthesis projects noise onto the set of images sharing these values. (11 October 2026)\n- Statistic it implies: beyond the spectrum, magnitude correlations across orientations and scales capture edges and lines, and the phase term captures their alignment across scales, which is exactly what separates a crack from colored noise. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): measure the kurtosis of each band of the residual and the correlation of magnitudes across scales; Gaussian like bands (kurtosis near 3, weak cross scale correlation) are solved by noise layers (2.5); heavy tailed bands with strong cross scale correlation hold sparse coherent features (cracks, chips, line edges) and must be solved by masks, curves or stamps, never by noise. (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.8 Gram matrices, Gatys, Ecker and Bethge 2015, and the sliced Wasserstein alternative (11 October 2026)\n\n- References: L. A. Gatys, A. S. Ecker, M. Bethge, \"Texture Synthesis Using Convolutional Neural Networks\", NeurIPS 2015, https://arxiv.org/abs/1505.07376; E. Heitz, K. Vanhoey, T. Chambon, L. Belcour, \"A Sliced Wasserstein Loss for Neural Texture Synthesis\", CVPR 2021, pp. 9412 to 9420, https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.07229 (abstracts read). (11 October 2026)\n- Mechanism: a texture is represented by Gram matrices, correlations between feature maps of a pretrained VGG at several depths; synthesis is gradient descent from white noise toward the exemplar Gram matrices; Heitz et al. point out the drawbacks of the Gram loss and compare full feature distributions instead, by projecting features on random directions and comparing sorted values (sliced Wasserstein distance). (11 October 2026)\n- Statistic it implies: second order (Gram) or complete (sliced Wasserstein) distributions of deep features, independent of position, multi scale through the depth of each layer. (11 October 2026)\n- Caveats for heights: VGG expects natural RGB images, so a height must be replicated in three channels and normalized; the statistic depends on pixel scale, so both images must share it; it is blind to exact positions by design; how well VGG features transfer to raw height maps is UNVERIFIED, since the published works compare renders. (11 October 2026)\n- Next layer guidance (PROPOSED): split the Gram or sliced Wasserstein distance by VGG depth; a discrepancy concentrated in shallow layers points to micro texture (glaze ripple, pinholes), in middle layers to meso features (chips, crack networks, motif lines), in deep layers to layout; the depth carrying the largest share picks the scale family of the next layer. (11 October 2026)\n\n### 2.9 Summary: which statistic chooses which next layer (11 October 2026)\n\n<table><tr><th>Technique</th><th>Statistic computed on reference residual and output residual</th><th>Diagnostic</th><th>Next layer it suggests</th><th>Date (11 October 2026)</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Efros and Leung</td><td>bidirectional nearest patch distance</td><td>clusters of uncovered reference patches</td><td>the feature of the largest cluster, at the window scale where matching fails</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Efros and Freeman</td><td>guide conditioned block matching</td><td>detail that no noise reproduces</td><td>semi procedural exemplar layer (M14)</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Wang tiles</td><td>spectral peaks, autocorrelation</td><td>spurious periodic peaks in the output</td><td>de-periodization by per tile random offsets or hextile</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heitz and Neyret</td><td>residual variance per region</td><td>output flatter than reference</td><td>variance preserving blend or quantile remap, not a new layer</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gabor and random phase noise</td><td>2D power spectrum</td><td>band and sector with the largest log deficit</td><td>noise layer with that center frequency, bandwidth and orientation</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Heeger and Bergen</td><td>histogram per pyramid band</td><td>band with the largest Wasserstein distance, sign of skew</td><td>ridge or pit layer at that scale</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Portilla and Simoncelli</td><td>band kurtosis, cross scale magnitude correlation</td><td>heavy tails with coherence across scales</td><td>masks, curves or stamps, never noise</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Gram or sliced Wasserstein</td><td>distance share per VGG depth</td><td>depth with the largest share</td><td>scale family: micro, meso or macro</td><td>11 October 2026</td></tr></table>\n\nAll these diagnostics are cheap (one FFT, a few Gaussian blurs, a histogram per band) and can run outside Houdini on an EXR export of the cooked output, which keeps every Copernicus cook within seconds as the project rules demand (PROPOSED). (11 October 2026)\nEOF\npython3 -I /private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-zenray--claude-Memory1/3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953/scratchpad/check_part.py /Users/zenray/.claude/tmp/tmp-zenray-height/dataset/parts/08_state_of_the_art_methods.md; echo \"exit=$?\"","description":"Append section 2 and validate the file format"}},"serverClassifierRequest":"49dc9536-d0aa-4570-9d4a-6d26726784b6","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011Cfvd5uU2XudRVEj9dJ3TC","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","attributionSkill":"zenray-houdini","type":"assistant","uuid":"cba0c322-63f0-4e9f-83d3-53752a42c773","timestamp":"2026-10-11T14:41:41.924Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/zenray/.claude/Memory1","sessionId":"3a9c2407-7dcc-4acd-baf2-90a94300e953","version":"2.1.295","gitBranch":"main"}
